<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hoysaleswara Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Hoysaleshvara Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hoysaleswara Temple at Halebidu, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra">Dwarasamudra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, is a twin-shrined temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> begun around 1121 during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a>. Built of soapstone on a star-shaped platform, it is covered with friezes and thousands of carved figures. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-ensembles-of-the-hoysalas">Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas</a> World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hoysaleswara-temple.jpg" alt="Hoysaleswara Temple" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karthikbs23 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoysaleshwara_temple_in_Monsoon.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
